        "heading": "Overcoming Peer Snobbery, Class Stereotypes, and Family Biases",
        "content": "\n          <p>External social pressure is one of the most common threats to cross-institutional romance. Coursemates in university hostels may make insensitive jokes about a partner attending a polytechnic, questioning why they did not choose a fellow university student. In some cases, status-conscious parents or extended family members echo outdated historical biases regarding polytechnic qualifications.</p>\n          <p>Surviving this pressure requires complete unity as a couple. If your partner attends a polytechnic, take pride in their technical achievements, artistic talents, and vocational competence. If your partner attends a university, celebrate their intellectual research and academic writing without feeling insecure about your own educational path.</p>\n          <p>Never permit friends or relatives to demean your partner’s institution in your presence. When you defend your partner’s dignity publicly, you build unshakeable trust and demonstrate that your love is anchored in genuine admiration rather than social prestige.</p>\n        "
